# Chezmoi Managed Home

## Core Rule

- Edit source in `{{ .chezmoi.sourceDir }}`, then apply; never edit managed home targets directly (apply overwrites them).
- Prefer adding unmanaged targets to chezmoi. Check ownership with `chezmoi managed <path>` or source mappings.

## Common Sources

- `~/.config/git/hooks/post-checkout` -> `dot_config/git/exact_hooks/executable_post-checkout`
- `~/.gitconfig` -> `dot_gitconfig.tmpl`
- `~/.zshrc` -> `dot_zshrc.tmpl`
- `~/airules` -> `exact_airules`
- `run_*` scripts -> `.chezmoiscripts/{posix,macos,linux,windows}/`

## Conventions

- `dot_foo` -> `~/.foo`; `dot_config/` -> `~/.config/`; `.tmpl` -> Go template; `executable_` -> executable target.
- `run_once_` / `run_after_` -> run by `chezmoi apply`.
- Use `.tmpl` only for host-specific values/secrets; prefer app env vars plus env templates when `${VAR}` expansion works.
- Use `exact_` only for fully source-owned directories, not app roots with credentials/state/dependencies; make managed subdirectories exact.

## Verify Edits

- Run `timeout 2 chezmoi diff` and `timeout 2 chezmoi apply -v` when practical; on timeout, tell the user to run `czapply` manually for pending input.

## Agent Behavior

- Keep reusable rules in `exact_airules/`; update `dot_config/opencode/AGENTS.md` for additions/renames/removals. Apply changed rules and all shared AGENTS.md targets.
- In this repo, add permanent tests, mocks, or fixtures only on request; use existing checks or temporary self-checks.
- Prefer configuration changes; explain maintenance costs before application patches/forks. Keep cleanup task-scoped unless broader refactoring is requested.
- Verify old files and service state before migration or cleanup; source definitions alone do not prove deployment. If a
  relevant host is inaccessible, ask instead of assuming. Keep cleanup minimal: assume one user/app state and one
  completed cleanup is enough. Mention repeat cleanup in the summary and ask before adding it.
- Before finishing tasks that run commands, check `git status --short` for unexpected generated files or edits.
- Before finishing tasks that spawn background processes, check `pty_list` and kill task-owned sessions.

## Packages

- `install/*file` (`npmfile`, `Brewfile`, `Wingetfile`, `uv-file`, `Gofile`, etc.) is authoritative; `make mise-packages` should prune unlisted globals.
- Before OS dependencies, check ignored packages and mise runtimes. Never override `IgnorePkg` without approval or duplicate a mise-owned runtime.
- Give each package one owner. Prefer cross-platform tool/language managers; use OS managers for OS-specific apps, system libraries, GUI apps, drivers, fonts, or unsuitable/unavailable tools.
- When moving/removing packages, update the old manager file and uninstall/prune from the current machine.
- Add packages to the matching managed file before installation, even when upstream docs prescribe global installs; one-off installs require explicit request.
- Winget: add ID to `install/Wingetfile`, then `make winget-packages`. Hand-run `winget install` only if that target is unavailable or being debugged, with
  `--silent --disable-interactivity --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements`.

## Cross-Platform

- Keep Arch/macOS/Win11 consistent where possible. Windows: `pwsh.exe` (PS 7), never `powershell.exe` (PS 5).
- Windows MinGW: use `winget install --id BrechtSanders.WinLibs.POSIX.UCRT --exact --silent --disable-interactivity --accept-package-agreements --accept-source-agreements`, not MSYS2 `pacman`.
- Check `.chezmoitemplates/env_vars_ps1` and `env_vars_posix`, including aliases and env-backed app config.
- Put simple aliases in `.chezmoidata.toml` `[sharedAliases]`, not shell templates.

## Scripts

- Prefer Python (available on every target) for nontrivial cross-platform alias utilities when compilation is unwanted.
